    "q-141479": {
      "dom_id": "step-q141479",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "Consider the polar curve $r={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta $ for $\\theta \\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$ Given that\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(\\theta )=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -{sin}^{3}⁡\\theta ,\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )=3{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\n$$\n\nand ${y}^{′}(\\theta )=0$ at $\\theta =0,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${x}^{′}(0)=0$\n\nII. ${lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=0$\n\nIII. The curve has a horizontal tangent at $\\theta =0$\n\n*Hint: It is not necessary to use L'Hopital's rule to evaluate the limit in part II.*",
      "worked_solution": "When a polar curve is differentiable at a point ${\\theta }_{0}$, there is a horizontal tangent at ${\\theta }_{0}$ if\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})}{{x}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})}=0\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nHowever, if the curve is continuous at ${\\theta }_{0}$ yet the derivative ${y}^{′}(x)$ takes the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}$ at this point, we say that the curve has a horizontal tangent at this point if\n\n$$\n{lim}_{\\theta \\to {\\theta }_{0}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=0.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're told that ${y}^{′}(0)=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Substituting $\\theta =0$ into the expression for ${x}^{′}(\\theta ),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(0) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0){cos}^{2}⁡(0)-{sin}^{3}⁡(0) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 0\\cdot 1-0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Computing the limit, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}(\\frac{3{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -{sin}^{3}⁡\\theta }) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot (2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}(\\frac{\\cancel{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\cdot 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\cancel{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\cdot (2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}(\\frac{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }) \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)}{2{cos}^{2}⁡(0)-{sin}^{2}⁡(0)} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\cdot 0\\cdot 1}{2\\cdot 1-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{2} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since the limit in part II equals $0,$ there curve has a horizontal tangent at $\\theta =0.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14272061/assets/01e875d1f34759e3fbd77b85f0ace57704180ac263b2acd68a6de82fb9d2b903.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
