    "q-143719": {
      "dom_id": "step-q143719",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "Consider the polar curve $r=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta $ for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).$ Given that \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(\\theta )={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta ({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -3{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ),\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ),\n$$\n\nand ${x}^{′}(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})=0,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${y}^{′}(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})$ is undefined\n\nII. ${lim}_{\\theta \\to 3\\pi /2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=0$\n\nIII. The curve has a horizontal tangent at $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$\n\n*Hint: It is not necessary to use L'Hopital's rule to evaluate the limit in part II.*",
      "worked_solution": "When a polar curve is differentiable at a point ${\\theta }_{0}$, there is a horizontal tangent at ${\\theta }_{0}$ if\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})}{{x}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})}=0\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nHowever, if the curve is continuous at ${\\theta }_{0}$ yet the derivative ${y}^{′}(x)$ takes the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}$ at this point, we say that the curve has a horizontal tangent at this point if\n\n$$\n{lim}_{\\theta \\to {\\theta }_{0}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=0.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're told that ${x}^{′}(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Substituting $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$ into the expression for ${y}^{′}(\\theta ),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{3\\pi }{2})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{3\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi ) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Computing the limit as $\\theta \\to \\frac{3\\pi }{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to 3\\pi /2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 3\\pi /2}\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 3\\pi /2}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta ({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -3{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 3\\pi /2}(\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta ({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -3{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 3\\pi /2}(\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}{{cos}^{\\cancel{2}}⁡\\theta ({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -3{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 3\\pi /2}(\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -3{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{3\\pi }{2})}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})({cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})-3{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}))} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot (-1)\\cdot (-1)}{0\\cdot (0-3\\cdot 1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{0} \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the limit in part II does *not* equal to $0,$ there curve does *not* have a horizontal tangent at $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$ In fact, it is a vertical tangent.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14272061/assets/79f623235864fb078ca07e428535a9fa96db72e327d4a533f43add37ef8801fd.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\"",
