    "q-145000": {
      "dom_id": "step-q145000",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "Consider the polar curve $r={cos}^{3}⁡\\theta $ for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).$ Given that \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(\\theta )=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta ){cos}^{3}⁡(\\theta ),\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )={cos}^{2}⁡(\\theta )({cos}^{2}⁡(\\theta )-3{sin}^{2}⁡(\\theta )),\n$$\n\nand ${x}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=0,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=0$\n\nII. ${lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\infty $\n\nIII. The curve has a vertical tangent at $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the following results.*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )\\to {0}^{-}\\,\\text{as}\\,\\theta \\to {(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{-} \\\\ -4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )\\to {0}^{+}\\,\\text{as}\\,\\theta \\to {(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{+}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "For a given polar curve, there is a vertical tangent at $\\theta ={\\theta }_{0}$ if\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})}{{x}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})}\n$$\n\nis infinite. This means that\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})\\ne 0\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})=0.\n$$\n\nHowever, if the curve is continuous at ${\\theta }_{0}$ yet the derivative takes the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}$ at this point, we say that the curve has a vertical tangent at this point if\n\n$$\n{lim}_{\\theta \\to {\\theta }_{0}^{-}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\pm \\infty \\,\\text{and}\\,{lim}_{\\theta \\to {\\theta }_{0}^{+}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\pm \\infty .\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're told that ${x}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Substituting $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ into the expression for ${y}^{′}(\\theta ),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & ={cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})({cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-3{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})) \\\\  & =0(0-3) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Computing the limit as $\\theta \\to {(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{+},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}(\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡(\\theta )({cos}^{2}⁡(\\theta )-3{sin}^{2}⁡(\\theta ))}{-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta ){cos}^{3}⁡(\\theta )}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}(\\frac{\\cancel{{cos}^{2}⁡(\\theta )}({cos}^{2}⁡(\\theta )-3{sin}^{2}⁡(\\theta ))}{-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta ){cos}^{\\cancel{3}}⁡(\\theta )}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}(\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡(\\theta )-3{sin}^{2}⁡(\\theta )}{-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the limit in the numerator, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}({cos}^{2}⁡(\\theta )-3{sin}^{2}⁡(\\theta )) & ={cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-3{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =0-3\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the second hint, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}(\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡(\\theta )-3{sin}^{2}⁡(\\theta )}{-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )}) \\\\  & =\\frac{-3}{{0}^{+}} \\\\  & =-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Using the calculations from part II, and the first hint, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}(\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡(\\theta )-3{sin}^{2}⁡(\\theta )}{-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )}) \\\\  & =\\frac{-3}{{0}^{-}} \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the limits of our derivative are both infinite, there is a vertical tangent to the curve at $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14272061/assets/8e0aded769ce741411085da3c68bc26b92c5568dd19e8e60f416615e5f849558.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"",
